  <static seed="1782" source="Contrib/PCC/BasicMath/Decimal/AddSubtractDecimals50.pg">
    <statement><p>Simplify without using a calculator.</p>
<p><m>\displaystyle{ -35 + 7.37 }</m></p>
<p><fillin characters="11" name="AnSwEr0001"/></p>
</statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>-27.63</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <solution><p>When we add a negative number with a positive number, we need to find the difference of their absolute values. When doing decimal subtractions, make sure to line up the decimal points. For the integer <m>-35</m>, the decimal point is at the end of the number, like in <m>-35.00</m></p>
<p><m>\displaystyle{\begin{aligned}[t]
   \amp \phantom{-}35.00 \\
 \amp \underline{-\phantom{3}7.37\phantom{ad}} \\
   \amp \phantom{-}27.63
\end{aligned}
    }</m></p>
<p>Next, since the absolute value of <m>-35</m> is bigger than that of <m>7.37</m>, the result is negative.</p>
<p><m>-35 + 7.37 = -27.63</m></p>
</solution>
